// One quiz's questions, loaded on demand by group-quizzes/loader.js.
(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-4-endo2-part2-quiz"]={"title":"Endocrine Physiology II — Part 2: Pancreas, Calcium & PTH (90 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which endocrine portion of the pancreas contains the hormone-producing cells?","choices":["Acini","Islets of Langerhans","Ampulla of Vater","Pancreatic ducts"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hormone-producing cells sit in the islets of Langerhans (endocrine pancreas). — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Which pancreatic cell produces insulin?","choices":["Alpha cell","PP cell","Delta cell","Beta cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta cells make insulin. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Which pancreatic cell produces glucagon?","choices":["Acinar cell","Beta cell","Delta cell","Alpha cell"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alpha cells make glucagon. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Somatostatin is produced by:","choices":["Alpha cells","Delta cells","Beta cells","PP cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Delta cells make somatostatin. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Pancreatic polypeptide is secreted by:","choices":["Alpha cells","PP cells","Delta cells","Beta cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. PP cells make pancreatic polypeptide. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Amylin is secreted together with:","choices":["Cortisol","Somatostatin","Glucagon","Insulin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Amylin is co-secreted with insulin from beta cells. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Insulin is classified as a:","choices":["Thyroid hormone","Catecholamine","Peptide hormone","Steroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Insulin is a peptide hormone. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Insulin circulates in plasma primarily:","choices":["Bound to albumin","Free (unbound)","Bound to TBG","Bound to CBG"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Insulin circulates free (unbound) in plasma. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Which laboratory value best reflects endogenous insulin production?","choices":["C-peptide","Glucose","Hemoglobin A1c","Glucagon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. C-peptide best reflects endogenous insulin production (co-secreted 1:1). — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Insulin receptors belong to which receptor family?","choices":["Ligand-gated ion channels","G-protein coupled receptors","Nuclear receptors","Tyrosine kinase receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The insulin receptor is a tyrosine kinase receptor. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Insulin increases glucose uptake primarily in:","choices":["Neurons","Skeletal muscle","Red blood cells","Brain"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Insulin drives glucose uptake mainly in skeletal muscle (GLUT4). — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Insulin does NOT significantly increase glucose uptake in the:","choices":["Adipose tissue","Cardiac muscle","Skeletal muscle","Brain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The brain takes up glucose insulin-independently. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"In skeletal muscle, insulin promotes:","choices":["Gluconeogenesis","Glycogen breakdown","Lipolysis","Glycogen synthesis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In skeletal muscle, insulin promotes glycogen synthesis. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"In the liver, insulin promotes:","choices":["Ketogenesis","Glycogen storage","Glucose release","Glycogenolysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the liver, insulin promotes glycogen storage. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Insulin inhibits:","choices":["Glycogen synthesis","Protein synthesis","Gluconeogenesis","Amino acid uptake"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Insulin inhibits gluconeogenesis. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"In adipose tissue insulin:","choices":["Stimulates hormone-sensitive lipase","Increases lipolysis","Releases triglycerides","Inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In adipose tissue insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase (blocks lipolysis). — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"The major effect of insulin on protein metabolism is:","choices":["Increased protein breakdown","Increased gluconeogenesis","Increased amino acid uptake and protein synthesis","Increased urea production only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Insulin increases amino acid uptake and protein synthesis. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"The overall function of insulin is best described as:","choices":["Emergency hormone","Catabolic hormone","Storage hormone","Stress hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Insulin is best described as the body's storage (anabolic) hormone. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Insulin deficiency results in:","choices":["Decreased fatty acids","Increased glycogen synthesis","Increased lipolysis","Increased protein synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Insulin deficiency releases the brake on fat → increased lipolysis. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"A severe lack of insulin results in increased production of:","choices":["Glycogen","Lactate","Cholesterol only","Ketone bodies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Severe insulin lack → excess lipolysis → increased ketone bodies (DKA). — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"The strongest stimulus for insulin secretion is:","choices":["Cortisol","Epinephrine","Increased blood glucose","Growth hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The strongest stimulus for insulin secretion is a rise in blood glucose. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Which amino acid strongly stimulates insulin secretion?","choices":["Tyrosine","Glycine","Lysine","Histidine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Among the options, lysine strongly stimulates insulin secretion (certain amino acids are secretagogues). — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Which gastrointestinal hormone increases insulin secretion before nutrients are fully absorbed?","choices":["ACTH","GLP-1","ADH","PTH"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. GLP-1 is the incretin that boosts insulin secretion. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"The incretin effect refers to:","choices":["Increased growth hormone after meals","Increased insulin secretion after oral glucose due to GI hormones","Increased cortisol after meals","Increased glucagon secretion during fasting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The incretin effect: oral glucose triggers more insulin than IV, due to gut hormones (GLP-1, GIP). — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Sulfonylurea drugs stimulate insulin secretion by:","choices":["Blocking calcium channels","Blocking insulin receptors","Closing ATP-sensitive potassium channels in beta cells","Activating glucagon receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sulfonylureas close ATP-sensitive K+ channels in beta cells, triggering insulin release. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Glucagon is produced by:","choices":["Alpha cells","Delta cells","PP cells","Beta cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Glucagon is produced by alpha cells. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Glucagon primarily acts on the:","choices":["Bone","Skeletal muscle","Brain","Liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Glucagon acts primarily on the liver. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"The primary purpose of glucagon is to:","choices":["Increase calcium absorption","Lower blood glucose","Increase blood glucose","Increase insulin secretion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glucagon's primary purpose is to increase blood glucose. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"Glucagon stimulates:","choices":["Fat storage","Protein synthesis","Glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis","Glycogen synthesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"⭐ Integration: A patient has been fasting for 18 hours. Which hormone would be expected to have the greatest increase?","choices":["Amylin","Insulin","Glucagon","GLP-1"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After 18 h fasting, glucagon rises the most to maintain glucose. — Endocrine Physiology II — Pancreatic Hormones lecture"},{"q":"The primary function of bone is:","choices":["Hormone storage only","Support, protection, and mineral storage","Fat storage","Mineral storage only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bone provides support, protection, and mineral (calcium) storage. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Which bone cell differentiates into osteoblasts?","choices":["Osteoprogenitor cell","Osteocyte","Osteoclast","Chondrocyte"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Osteoprogenitor cells differentiate into osteoblasts. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Which bone cell is responsible for producing osteoid?","choices":["Osteoclast","Osteocyte","Osteoblast","Fibroblast"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Osteoblasts produce osteoid (bone matrix). — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Which bone cell maintains mature bone tissue?","choices":["Osteocyte","Osteoclast","Osteoblast","Osteoprogenitor"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Osteocytes maintain mature bone tissue. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Which bone cell is responsible for bone resorption?","choices":["Osteoblast","Osteocyte","Chondroblast","Osteoclast"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Osteoclasts resorb bone. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Osteoclasts are derived primarily from:","choices":["Chondrocytes","Osteoblasts","Mesenchymal stem cells","Monocytes/macrophages"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Osteoclasts derive from monocytes/macrophages. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Approximately what percentage of the body's calcium is stored in bone?","choices":["50%","90%","75%","99%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ~99% of body calcium is stored in bone. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Only about what percentage of total body calcium is rapidly exchangeable?","choices":["1%","20%","5%","10%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Only ~1% of body calcium is rapidly exchangeable. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Normal plasma calcium concentration is approximately:","choices":["9.4 mg/dL","12.5 mg/dL","4.5 mg/dL","7.0 mg/dL"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Normal plasma calcium is ~9.4 mg/dL. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"The major mineral stored in bone is:","choices":["Sodium chloride","Magnesium","Hydroxyapatite (calcium-phosphate)","Potassium phosphate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bone mineral is hydroxyapatite (calcium-phosphate). — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Vitamin D synthesis begins in the:","choices":["Intestine","Liver","Skin","Kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin D synthesis begins in the skin (UV on 7-dehydrocholesterol). — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"After being produced in the skin, vitamin D is first converted in the:","choices":["Pancreas","Kidney","Bone","Liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The first hydroxylation (25-OH) occurs in the liver. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"The active form of vitamin D is produced primarily in the:","choices":["Skin","Thyroid","Liver","Kidney"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Active vitamin D (1-hydroxylation) is made in the kidney. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"The active form of vitamin D is:","choices":["Cholecalciferol","1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D (Calcitriol)","25-hydroxy vitamin D","Ergocalciferol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The active form is 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol). — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Vitamin D acts primarily on the:","choices":["Spleen","Brain","Intestine","Heart"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin D acts mainly on the intestine. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Vitamin D primarily increases intestinal absorption of:","choices":["Sodium and potassium","Iron and magnesium","Calcium and phosphate","Glucose and amino acids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin D increases intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone stimulates activation of vitamin D in the kidney?","choices":["Calcitonin","Insulin","Cortisol","Parathyroid hormone (PTH)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. PTH stimulates renal 1-hydroxylation (activation) of vitamin D. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Parathyroid hormone is secreted by the:","choices":["Parathyroid glands","Thyroid follicular cells","Pancreatic beta cells","Adrenal cortex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. PTH comes from the parathyroid glands. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"The primary stimulus for PTH secretion is:","choices":["High vitamin D","Low plasma calcium","High plasma calcium","High phosphate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low plasma calcium is the primary stimulus for PTH. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"The overall effect of PTH is to:","choices":["Lower calcium","Lower sodium","Increase calcium","Increase potassium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. PTH's net effect is to increase plasma calcium. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"PTH stimulates bone by:","choices":["Inhibiting osteoclasts","Increasing bone resorption","Increasing bone formation only","Increasing collagen synthesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. PTH increases bone resorption (via osteoclast activation). — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"In the kidney, PTH increases:","choices":["Sodium excretion","Glucose filtration","Calcium excretion","Calcium reabsorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In the kidney PTH increases calcium reabsorption. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"PTH decreases renal reabsorption of:","choices":["Potassium","Phosphate","Calcium","Sodium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. PTH decreases phosphate reabsorption (phosphaturia). — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"PTH indirectly increases intestinal calcium absorption by:","choices":["Activating vitamin D","Increasing calcitonin","Increasing glucagon","Increasing insulin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. PTH indirectly boosts intestinal calcium absorption by activating vitamin D. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Which combination best describes PTH?","choices":["↑ Calcium, ↓ Phosphate","↓ Calcium, ↑ Phosphate","↑ Calcium, ↑ Phosphate","↓ Calcium, ↓ Phosphate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. PTH raises calcium and lowers phosphate. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Calcitonin is produced by:","choices":["Adrenal medulla","Parathyroid chief cells","Thyroid follicular cells","Thyroid parafollicular (C) cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Calcitonin comes from thyroid parafollicular (C) cells. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Calcitonin primarily lowers plasma calcium by:","choices":["Increasing PTH secretion","Inhibiting osteoclast activity","Increasing intestinal calcium absorption","Increasing osteoclast activity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calcitonin lowers calcium by inhibiting osteoclasts. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Compared with PTH, calcitonin has:","choices":["Greater effects on the kidney","Little physiological importance in healthy adults","Greater effects on vitamin D activation","A major role in adult calcium homeostasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calcitonin has little physiological importance in healthy adults. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is considered the MOST important regulator of plasma calcium?","choices":["Calcitonin","Vitamin D","Insulin","Parathyroid hormone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. PTH is the most important regulator of plasma calcium. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"⭐ Integration: A patient develops severe hypocalcemia. Which sequence is MOST likely?","choices":["↓ Vitamin D → ↓ PTH","↑ PTH → ↑ active Vitamin D → ↑ intestinal calcium absorption","↓ PTH → ↓ Vitamin D → ↓ calcium absorption","↑"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypocalcemia → PTH rises → activates vitamin D → increases intestinal calcium absorption. — Endocrine Physiology II — Bone, Calcium & PTH lecture"},{"q":"A patient has destruction of the adrenal cortex (Addison disease). Which hormone would be MOST decreased?","choices":["Norepinephrine","Dopamine","Epinephrine","Cortisol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Addison destroys the cortex, so cortisol (a cortical steroid) drops. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Which electrolyte abnormality is most consistent with primary adrenal insufficiency?","choices":["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia","Hypercalcemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adrenal insufficiency → low aldosterone → hyperkalemia. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient has excess aldosterone secretion. Which combination is expected?","choices":["Hypotension + Hyponatremia","Hyperglycemia + Hypercalcemia","Hypotension + Hyperkalemia","Hypertension + Hypokalemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Excess aldosterone → hypertension + hypokalemia. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient has chronically elevated cortisol levels. Which metabolic effect is expected?","choices":["Increased insulin sensitivity","Decreased protein breakdown","Hypoglycemia","Increased gluconeogenesis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic cortisol excess increases gluconeogenesis (hyperglycemia). — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Long-term corticosteroid therapy increases the risk of:","choices":["Infection","Enhanced immune function","Hypoglycemia","Hyperkalemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Long-term steroids suppress immunity, raising infection risk. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient with Cushing syndrome is MOST likely to have:","choices":["Hyperglycemia","Hyponatremia","Hypotension","Weight loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cushing → hyperglycemia (cortisol is diabetogenic). — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Excess cortisol suppresses secretion of:","choices":["Glucagon","PTH","ACTH","Insulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Excess cortisol suppresses ACTH via negative feedback. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is primarily responsible for the body's response to long-term stress?","choices":["Calcitonin","Cortisol","Insulin","Epinephrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cortisol governs the long-term stress response. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient eats excessive black licorice daily. Which finding is most likely?","choices":["Hypertension with hypokalemia","Hypotension","Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Excess licorice mimics aldosterone → hypertension with hypokalemia. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"The physiological effect of licorice resembles excess:","choices":["Aldosterone","Glucagon","Insulin","PTH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Licorice produces apparent mineralocorticoid (aldosterone-like) excess. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient with Type 1 diabetes lacks:","choices":["Glucagon","Somatostatin","Insulin","Cortisol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Type 1 diabetes = lack of insulin. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"The major cause of ketoacidosis in Type 1 diabetes is:","choices":["Excess lipolysis and ketone production","Increased insulin secretion","Excess calcium","Excess glycogen synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Insulin deficiency drives excess lipolysis and ketone production → DKA. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone opposes nearly every major action of insulin?","choices":["Glucagon","Oxytocin","Prolactin","ACTH"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Glucagon opposes nearly all insulin actions. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Following a carbohydrate-rich meal, which hormone should increase the most?","choices":["Cortisol","Epinephrine","Glucagon","Insulin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After a carbohydrate meal, insulin rises the most. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient fasts for 24 hours. Which hormone should be highest?","choices":["GLP-1","Amylin","Glucagon","Insulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. After 24 h fasting, glucagon is highest. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone directly stimulates glycogen breakdown in the liver during fasting?","choices":["Insulin","Calcitonin","Glucagon","Prolactin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glucagon drives hepatic glycogenolysis during fasting. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient has a beta-cell tumor producing excessive insulin. Which finding is expected?","choices":["Hypoglycemia","Hypernatremia","Hypercalcemia","Hyperglycemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An insulinoma (excess insulin) causes hypoglycemia. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Somatostatin inhibits secretion of:","choices":["Glucagon only","Both insulin and glucagon","Insulin only","Cortisol only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Somatostatin inhibits both insulin and glucagon. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is considered the body's primary \"storage hormone\"?","choices":["Epinephrine","Insulin","Cortisol","Glucagon"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Insulin is the primary storage (anabolic) hormone. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Which hormone is considered the body's primary fasting hormone?","choices":["Oxytocin","Insulin","Glucagon","Calcitonin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glucagon is the primary fasting hormone. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient has severe hypocalcemia. Which hormone should increase first?","choices":["Insulin","PTH","Calcitonin","Cortisol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypocalcemia → PTH rises first. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"PTH raises calcium by:","choices":["Decreasing vitamin D activation","Increasing bone resorption","Increasing urinary calcium excretion","Increasing calcitonin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. PTH raises calcium partly by increasing bone resorption. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Activated vitamin D primarily increases calcium absorption in the:","choices":["Liver","Bone","Intestine","Kidney"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Active vitamin D increases calcium absorption in the intestine. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Calcitonin primarily inhibits:","choices":["Osteoblasts","Osteocytes","Osteoclasts","Fibroblasts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Calcitonin inhibits osteoclasts. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient has chronic vitamin D deficiency. Which finding is most likely?","choices":["Increased intestinal calcium absorption","Increased calcitonin secretion","Hypercalcemia","Decreased intestinal calcium absorption"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin D deficiency → decreased intestinal calcium absorption. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism develops most commonly because of:","choices":["Hypercalcemia","Chronic kidney disease","Hyperinsulinemia","Hyperaldosteronism"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Secondary hyperparathyroidism most often results from chronic kidney disease. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"⭐ Integration: A patient has hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis. Which hormone abnormality best explains these findings?","choices":["↓ PTH","↑ Cortisol only","↑ Aldosterone","↑ Insulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypertension + hypokalemia + metabolic alkalosis = aldosterone excess. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient develops hyperglycemia, central obesity, and muscle wasting. Which hormone is most likely elevated?","choices":["Glucagon","PTH","Cortisol","Insulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hyperglycemia + central obesity + muscle wasting = cortisol excess (Cushing). — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"A patient has low calcium, high PTH, low active vitamin D, and chronic kidney disease. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Primary hyperparathyroidism","Type 1 diabetes","Addison disease","Secondary hyperparathyroidism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Low calcium, high PTH, low active vitamin D with CKD = secondary hyperparathyroidism. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"},{"q":"⭐ Final Integration: A patient presents with chronic fasting, low insulin, high glucagon, elevated cortisol, and elevated epinephrine. Which metabolic state is most likely?","choices":["Catabolism with increased glucose production and fat utilization","Glycogen storage","Hyperinsulinemia","Anabolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic fasting with low insulin and high counter-regulatory hormones = catabolism with increased glucose production and fat use. — Endocrine Physiology II — Clinical Integration lecture"}]};
